  "Question": "In the set \\(\\{1,2,2\\}\\), the element 2 is written twice. According to the distinctness property, can it count as 2 different elements?",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "Yes, as long as both are written out.",
    "B": "Yes, but parentheses must be added.",
    "C": "No, the elements are distinct.",
    "D": "Only infinite sets do not allow this."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "The elements of a set are distinct. \\(\\{1,2,2\\}\\) and \\(\\{1,2\\}\\) are the same set, and the repeated 2 counts as only 1 element.",
